---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, AUTHOR, POLICY } from '../data/site';
import '../styles/article.css';

const schema = [{
  '@type': 'Organization',
  '@id': `${SITE.url}${POLICY.editorial}#team`,
  name: 'Intelleo Editorial',
  url: `${SITE.url}${POLICY.editorial}`,
  parentOrganization: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` },
  // Accountable for the workflow and its standards.
  member: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#person-sandeep` },
  publishingPrinciples: `${SITE.url}${POLICY.editorialPolicy}`,
}];
---
<Base
  title="Intelleo Editorial — How Our Articles Are Made"
  description="How Intelleo's automated editorial workflow finds sources, writes, fact-checks and publishes articles, and who is accountable for it."
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Intelleo Editorial</span>
      <h1>How our articles are made</h1>
      <p>Articles bylined “Intelleo Editorial” are written by an automated workflow. This page explains it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section style="padding-top:24px">
    <div class="wrap article-inner prose">
      <h2>Who is accountable?</h2>
      <p>
        {AUTHOR.name} ({AUTHOR.jobTitle}) owns the workflow, its rules and its output. Questions and
        corrections go to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where does the material come from?</h2>
      <p>
        The workflow collects publisher feeds, release notes from open-source projects, and web search results
        several times a day. Each source is scored for relevance, evidence, authority and freshness before it
        can lead a story. News must rest on a primary source, and every article draws on at least three sources
        from at least two independent organisations.
      </p>
      <p>
        When an article describes Intelleo's own experience, that claim must come from a case study or field note
        we have published under <a href={POLICY.experience}>Experience</a>. The workflow cannot claim experience
        that is not documented there.
      </p>

      <h2>How is an article written and checked?</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>A research model prepares a brief: the reader, the decision they face, and the sources to use.</li>
        <li>A writing model drafts the article and a ledger tying each material claim to an exact quoted passage.</li>
        <li>Software checks that every quoted passage really appears in its source, that consequential claims are
          independently corroborated, and that every link points to a listed source.</li>
        <li>A different model fact-checks the full article against the source passages and blocks anything unsupported.</li>
        <li>An editorial review rejects drafts that are generic, repetitive or do not answer their own headline.</li>
        <li>A quality gate re-fetches every cited source, rejects near-duplicates of earlier articles, and caps
          output at one article a day.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A draft that fails any step is revised or discarded. On some days nothing is published.</p>

      <h2>What happens when something is wrong?</h2>
      <p>
        The workflow re-reads the sources of recent articles daily. When a source shows a verified factual error,
        the article is corrected and a dated note is added. All corrections are listed on the
        <a href={POLICY.corrections}>corrections page</a>. See the <a href={POLICY.editorialPolicy}>editorial policy</a>
        for the full rules.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
